v1.11.0
==============
* BREAKING: record/replay removed -- core/cassette, the `ipandora cassette` command, and eight `run` flags (--record, --replay, --verify, --cassette, --cassette-dir, --on-exhausted, --max-cassette-age, --allow-diff). A general test framework should not own mocking; a suite that needs a stubbed response can use `responses` or `httpretty`. Every HTTP request also passed through a record/replay check whether or not anything was recording; the transport adapter is a plain HTTPAdapter again. See docs/design/07 for how to restore it
* BREAKING: Source.ONCHAIN removed -- a Web3 dimension hardcoded into the general assertion layer. Check.src is a free string, so a suite can still tag `src='onchain'` and the report groups by it the same way. Source.DIFF went with record/replay
* BREAKING: data reporting is gone. The upload path collected every response, batched it on a background thread and then discarded it -- the request itself was commented out and called into `pandora_user`, which does not exist in this repo. Removed MultiHandle, UploadApiInfo, InnerPlugin, Runtime.Host/Mysql/Email, Option.only_api, and the mysql/file/email/addressesTo/report config sections
* BREAKING: Robot Framework support removed. utils/log.py imported `robot.api` unguarded, which made robotframework a hard requirement for importing the package at all, long after the adapter was archived. Also removed robotlogparser, the get_test_report MCP tool, Runtime.Frame.is_rf and the Robot branches in utils/variable.py
* BREAKING: removed orphan modules with no consumer -- common/uuidutils.py (UUIDFactory), common/timeutils.py, utils/filewriter.py, utils/niceline.py, core/base/classwrap/{singleton,attrvaluesplit}.py, core/base/repository/, common/mysqlaction.py
* BREAKING: eight dependencies that nothing imported are no longer installed -- websockets, bs4, six, tabulate, retrying, assertpy, pymysql, twine. pendulum went with timeutils; psutil is now the optional `system` extra and imported lazily
* every remaining dependency is version-bounded on both sides: a dependency that silently moves across a major makes a run's result depend on when it was installed
* add JUnit XML output, rendered from the same ReportData as the HTML report. `ipandora run` writes report.xml beside every report; --junit-xml puts a copy where CI expects it
* a collection failure now emits a real failing testcase in the XML: `tests="0" failures="0"` is indistinguishable from a clean run to every CI there is
* BLOCKED and GAP have no JUnit equivalent and are written as <skipped>, with the real status kept in the type attribute, the message and a property
* strip characters XML 1.0 forbids -- they cannot be escaped, and one stray byte in one assertion message would cost the whole run its report
* add @pytest.mark.flaky(reason=...) and `ipandora run --exclude-flaky`. Excluded cases are still named in the summary: a flaky marker nobody is reminded of is a failure that has been parked
* add scripts/mutation.py -- mutation testing, scoped to one module and its tests, with a score and --min-score for gating
* smoke_import now passes against a minimal install, excusing only the modules whose optional extra is genuinely missing
* BREAKING: json_matches removed, replaced by field() -- tests are Python, so judge fields with Python operators: `field(r, 'data.fee') <= 100`, `.is_in()`, `.contains()`, `.startswith()`, `.matches()`, `.len()`. Each comparison returns a Check, so assert_all still reports every failure at once. json_matches was never on PyPI
* BREAKING: ResponseHandler.filter takes a Python predicate instead of operator dicts -- `filter(lambda x: x['i'] > 3)`. Passing `{'$gt': 3}` raises with the equivalent lambda. Keyword equality stays, but is now strict: it compared str(a) == str(b), so '0' matched 0 and 'True' matched True. utils/match.py's Compare and DictMatcher are gone
* comparisons are strict: values compare as their own type -- the JSON string "12" is not greater than 9, and an API that switched numbers to strings is a finding, not something to coerce away; bool never equals a number (json_equals too); nothing is truncated. The operator dicts got all three wrong: `{'$eq': 5}` passed on 5.9, `{'$eq': True}` passed on the string 'false', and `{'__class__': 5}` passed on anything
* anything field() cannot judge -- a missing field, mismatched types, an invalid regex -- is a failed Check with the reason, never an exception; and a bare field used as a bool raises, so `if field(r, 'x'):` cannot be silently always-true
* config is read from the suite's own conf/ (./conf, or $IPANDORA_CONFIG_DIR) over the package defaults: conf/config.yaml for the project, conf/<env>.yaml per run. Previously both were looked up only inside the installed package, so configuring a suite meant editing site-packages. The old location still loads, with a warning
* fix: an env overlay replaced whole sections (shallow dict.update) -- `ai: {enabled: true}` dropped `ai.model`. Layers now merge recursively
* fix: an env overlay was never undone. In one process -- the MCP server -- a run with env=staging followed by env=prod (no prod.yaml) or by no env at all still used staging's settings. Each run's overlay is now removed when the run ends, pass or fail
* fix: `-e` naming a file that does not exist logged at debug level, so a typo ran the whole suite on defaults under the requested env's name. It is now a warning listing every path searched
* fix: the crypto key landed inside the tracked package tree and three separate gates failed to recognise it; it now lives in ~/.ipandora/crypto at 0700/0600
* fix: AESCryptographer held a base64 str where AES wants raw bytes after generating a key, so the first run of a fresh install always raised
* fix: MANIFEST.in named the distribution instead of the package path; scripts/check_packaging.py now checks the built wheel, because whether data files reach it depends on the setuptools version doing the build

v1.10.0
==============
* add core/report: build report data from a run, then render it -- HTML and JSON come from the same model
* strip secrets when the data is built, not when it is rendered; masking in a template would protect one view and leave the JSON behind it readable
* redaction masks on two independent signals, field name and value shape, so a key pasted into a free-text error message is caught even though it has no field name
* pass rate counts conclusive cases only -- a suite that skips half its cases is not 100% healthy
* report separates cases that told us nothing (skipped/blocked/gap/error) from real results, and shows how many failures actually implicate the system under test
* Status carries BLOCKED and GAP; neither is inferred from a run, since a run cannot report an assertion nobody wrote
* HTML autoescapes: failure messages contain whatever the system under test returned
* add build_report MCP tool
* an unrecognised outcome maps to ERROR rather than PASS

v1.9.0
==============
* add core/triage: rule-based failure classification -- defect, contract, environment, data, harness -- always on, deterministic, free
* separate failures that implicate the system under test from those that implicate our harness, data or network; conflating them is what teaches people to ignore red builds
* classification reuses Check.src recorded at assertion time: all-schema failures mean contract drift, derived means business logic
* run_tests and explain_failure now carry the classification and a next step per failure
* add optional LLM root-cause analysis in ai/triage.py, registered through a hook so core/ never imports ai/; it adds prose only and never decides a category
* add ipandora.ai.enable()/disable(); importing the package is not enough to start spending
* fix Runtime.Ai.max_calls_per_run being impossible to set to 0 -- the falsy check treated a configured 0 as unset and restored the default, disabling the strictest budget setting
* rewrite scripts/check_layering.py to parse imports with AST; the previous text grep flagged documentation that merely mentions ipandora.ai, which core/triage necessarily does
* strip pytest's (file, lineno, ...) wrapper from skip messages, which pointed at the assertion collector rather than anything useful

v1.8.0
==============
* add core/runner: run(selector, env) -> RunResult, callable as a library
* MCP run_tests/explain_failure/list_runs go through it; run_tests no longer shells out to pytest and parses stdout
* output forks by audience: summary() carries failure names and assertion messages for an agent, full tracebacks stay in the run store behind explain_failure(run_id)
* runs are archived by run_id under ~/.ipandora/runs (override with IPANDORA_RUNS_DIR), pruned to the last 50
* use --import-mode=importlib so run() can be called repeatedly in one process; the default mode aborts on a second run over same-named test files
* a selector that matches nothing, or any pytest exit code meaning the run did not happen, now reports ok=False instead of reading as "everything passed"
* run(quiet=True) suppresses pytest output, and the MCP server routes logging to stderr under stdio transport -- both would otherwise corrupt the JSON-RPC stream
* add log_to_stderr() for callers that own stdout

v1.7.0
==============
* Runtime.Case is now thread-local: steps used to live in a process-global dict keyed by a single process-global case name, so under any concurrency every thread's steps landed under whichever name was set last
* reading Runtime.Case.steps no longer pops -- the first reader used to empty it and a second read silently returned []; use drain_steps() to take them, clear() to reset
* MultiHandle buffers per instance under a lock; the buffer used to be a class attribute shared by every instance and mutated unsynchronised from both the producer and the background thread
* MultiHandle's background thread waits on an Event instead of busy-looping on sleep, so stop() takes effect immediately
* deduplicate the atexit flush registration, which two package __init__ modules were each registering
* add Runtime.reset() to drop memoised config, restoring defaults snapshotted at import; config overrides used to leak process-wide with no way to undo them

v1.6.0
==============
* fix response parsing: a single-object payload was torn into its field values, null and scalar data raised TypeError, and a string body was shredded into characters
* replace the namedtuple conversion with JsonObject, a dict that also supports attribute access -- resp.data.origin still works and resp.data['x-req-id'] now does too
* remove the silent degradation where a Python-keyword or dashed JSON key made json_to_obj fall back to a plain dict, so the parsed type depended on the payload's key names and filter() then raised AttributeError
* non-JSON bodies are returned as-is (str for text, bytes for binary) instead of being wrapped in a fake object
* filter() skips fieldless items instead of raising, and accepts dicts, JsonObject and namedtuples alike
* fetch_one() returns None rather than [] when there is no data
* __len__ no longer calls the consuming fetch_all(), so len() has no side effects and does not clear an applied filter
* AttributeError on a missing field now lists the fields that are present

v1.5.0
==============
* add core/protocol/http/transport.py -- TransportPolicy, retry adapter, error translation
* TLS verification now defaults to on; it was forced off for every https request, hiding certificate problems the suite should catch
* every request now carries a timeout (10s connect / 30s read by default); previously ApiOption defaulted to 0 and nothing applied it, so a hung endpoint hung the suite forever
* add retry with exponential backoff, restricted to idempotent methods and transient statuses (429/502/503/504); 500 is not retried
* transport failures raise HttpTimeoutError / HttpConnectionError carrying url, method and elapsed, and also inherit the matching requests exception
* mount an HTTPAdapter with configurable pool sizing on every session
* add http section to config.yaml and Runtime.Http
* fix SessionManager check-then-act happening outside the lock
* fix SessionManager acquire/release without try/finally, which deadlocked the RLock permanently on any exception
* fix deprecated current_thread().getName()

v1.4.0
==============
* add core/assertion -- the framework previously had no assertion layer at all, so a 500 response passed silently
* Check is a value, not an exception: assert_all evaluates every judgement and reports all failures in one run
* require() skips instead of failing, so unmet preconditions stay distinguishable from real defects
* http checks: status_ok, status_is, header_is, json_has, json_equals, json_matches, schema_conforms
* jsonpath accessor reads the raw body, sidestepping ResponseHandler.fetch_all() (returns key names for single-object payloads, raises on null/scalar)
* json_matches reuses the existing Compare operators; all comparators stay deterministic and offline
* declare jsonschema explicitly (was only a transitive dependency of mcp)
* test_http_get now asserts status, which it never did before

v1.3.0
==============
* archive core/engine/generator stack (2737 LOC) -- removes the duplicate case/testcase data models, the unwired v2 robot render path, and the orphan repository modules; preserved at tag v1.1.0-archive
* archive run/ scaffold subcommands (ipandoracommand, commands/project) and the duplicate runner.py script
* remove hardcoded credentials from Runtime.Host; report host now comes from conf/config.yaml
* remove Compare.cmp_semantic -- assertions must stay deterministic and offline
* move ai/ out of core/ and default ai.enabled to false; add max_calls_per_run budget cap
* trim MCP server to get_test_report; run_tests/explain_failure deferred until core/runner and core/triage exist
* add CI (import smoke, test matrix, removable-ai gate, layering and secret checks)
* add pre-commit config with secret scanning
* fix Log.redInfo typo that crashed the CLI when no subcommand was given
* fix UploadApiInfo building its URL at class-definition time
* correct python_requires to >=3.10 (code already used PEP 604 unions)
* normalize runtime.py indentation to 4 spaces

